\description{
Converts the nested list returned by the 'Scopus' Search API into a flat,
predictable \link[tibble:tibble]{tibble} with one row per record. This shape is
the common currency of the package. Both \code{\link[=scopus_fetch]{scopus_fetch()}} and
\code{\link[=scopus_fetch_plan]{scopus_fetch_plan()}} return it, and the DOI, comparison and export helpers
all consume it.
}
\details{
The 'Scopus' API signals an empty result set with a single sentinel entry that
carries an \code{error} field and no identifier. This is detected and turned into a
zero-row result, with no spurious record in it, while a genuine record that also
carries a per-entry \code{error} annotation is kept.

What \code{authors} holds depends on the view. The Search API's \code{dc:creator}
field is the first author alone under either view, in the indexed form
'Scopus' uses (\code{"Zhang F."}). The author list arrives only under
\code{view = "COMPLETE"}, as the \code{author} objects Elsevier's views table lists
for that view alone. A \code{STANDARD} record therefore names its first author,
and a tally of \code{STANDARD} authors counts first authorships. A harvest
records its view in the \code{view} attribute (see \code{\link[=scopus_fetch]{scopus_fetch()}}), and
\code{\link[=scopus_top]{scopus_top()}} and \code{\link[=as_bibliometrix]{as_bibliometrix()}} say once per session that a set
retrieved under \code{STANDARD} names first authors only. Under \code{COMPLETE}, each
author is written as \code{"Surname, Given"}, as the surname alone when the given
name is missing or empty, or as the indexed name when the surname is
missing. Exact duplicates are dropped. The API lists at most 100 authors per
record, and its \code{author-count} field gives the total. A record whose list
names nobody falls back to \code{dc:creator}. The Python twin builds the same
string from pybliometrics' \code{author_names} in the common case, and parts
company at two edges. Where an author object lacks a \code{surname} or
\code{given-name} key, pybliometrics builds no list, so that twin keeps the first
author while this one lists every author. Where a surname is null or empty,
pybliometrics writes the given name after a bare comma, and this package uses
the indexed name.

Author keywords are only ever present under \code{view = "COMPLETE"}; the
\code{STANDARD} view (the default throughout the package) never includes them,
and \code{authkeywords} is not added to the output at all in that case, so
existing code that inspects the column names of a \code{STANDARD}-view result is
unaffected. Even under \code{COMPLETE} view, some 'Scopus' API keys do not return
populated author keywords (this was observed directly against a live,
otherwise fully-entitled key during development, on documents that do carry
author keywords in 'Scopus' itself); if your own keywords come back all
\code{NA}, the field is most likely gated by your account's entitlement rather
than genuinely absent, and is worth raising with your 'Scopus'/Elsevier
account contact.
}
\section{Extended fields}{

Elsevier's \href{https://dev.elsevier.com/sc_search_views.html}{Search views table}
lists every field \code{fields = "extended"} reads under both views, except the
abstract (\code{dc:description}), which only the \code{COMPLETE} view carries. They
arrive in the responses a retrieval already pays for, so they cost no extra
quota. A \code{COMPLETE} harvest returns up to 25 abstracts with each request,
while \code{\link[=scopus_abstract]{scopus_abstract()}} spends one request per record from the separate
Abstract Retrieval quota.

\code{doc_type} holds the code the \code{DOCTYPE()} search field takes. Elsevier's
\href{https://dev.elsevier.com/sc_search_tips.html}{search tips} list the codes
with their names: \code{ar} for an article, \code{re} a review, \code{cp} a conference
paper, \code{er} an erratum and others. A retracted item carries \code{tb}, which that
list leaves out. The 'Scopus' Content Coverage Guide (section 3.1) defines
an erratum as a report of an error, correction or retraction of a previously
published paper, so \code{er} covers retraction notices as well as corrections.
The guide gives an article the type Retracted once a retraction notice for
it is published. These codes are the labels 'Scopus' assigns, and they do
not screen a set for retractions, since 'Scopus' does not label every
retracted item (Ortega and Delgado-Quirós, 2024). Check a set against the
\href{https://www.crossref.org/documentation/retrieve-metadata/retraction-watch/}{Retraction Watch database},
which Crossref makes openly available, before relying on it.
\link[=summary.scopus_records]{summary()} reports how many records 'Scopus'
labels \code{er} and \code{tb}. Errata can also be left out of the search itself with
\verb{AND NOT DOCTYPE(er)}, joined by \code{\link[=scopus_query]{scopus_query()}}, which brackets the rest of
the query first, since 'Scopus' applies AND NOT last (see \emph{Examples}).

\code{open_access} holds the code 'Scopus' sends in its \code{openaccess} field.
Elsevier's Scopus APIs Getting Started Guide (section 17) illustrates its
values with 0 for a record that is not open access, 1 for gold and 2 for
green. The column therefore holds a status code, which can take values
other than 0 and 1.
}

\examples{
# An entry in the shape the Search API returns it. The fields are those of
# a real article, taken from the bundled `example_records`, which stands in
# for a harvest because 'Scopus' records may not be redistributed. It
# carries no 'Scopus' identifier, so `dc:identifier` is absent and
# `scopus_id` comes back NA, as it does for any unidentified record.
raw <- list(entry = list(
  list(
    `prism:doi` = "10.1021/am509065d",
    `dc:title` = "Flexible and Stackable Laser-Induced Graphene Supercapacitors",
    `dc:creator` = "Zhiwei Peng",
    `prism:publicationName` = "ACS Applied Materials & Interfaces",
    `prism:coverDate` = "2015-01-13",
    `citedby-count` = "469"
  )
))
scopus_records(raw, query = "TITLE-ABS-KEY(graphene supercapacitor)")

# Under COMPLETE view an entry may also carry author keywords, which the
# Search API returns in its own " | "-delimited form. The bundled corpus
# holds no keywords, so the ones below are illustrative.
raw_complete <- list(entry = list(
  list(
    `prism:doi` = "10.1021/am509065d",
    `dc:title` = "Flexible and Stackable Laser-Induced Graphene Supercapacitors",
    authkeywords = "graphene | supercapacitor | energy storage"
  )
))
scopus_records(raw_complete, view = "COMPLETE")

# fields = "extended" keeps the fields both views send beyond the core ones.
# The volume, issue, pages and print ISSN below are the article's own. The
# document type and source type are illustrative, since the bundled corpus
# came from OpenAlex and records no 'Scopus' labels.
raw_extended <- list(entry = list(
  list(
    `prism:doi` = "10.1021/am509065d",
    `dc:title` = "Flexible and Stackable Laser-Induced Graphene Supercapacitors",
    subtype = "ar",
    subtypeDescription = "Article",
    `prism:aggregationType` = "Journal",
    `prism:volume` = "7",
    `prism:issueIdentifier` = "5",
    `prism:pageRange` = "3414-3419",
    `prism:issn` = "19448244"
  )
))
extended <- scopus_records(raw_extended, fields = "extended")
as.data.frame(extended)[, c("doc_type", "volume", "issue", "pages", "issn")]

# Errata can be left out of the search itself. scopus_query() brackets the
# rest of the query, since 'Scopus' applies AND NOT last.
scopus_query(
  scopus_query("graphene", "graphite", .op = "OR", .field = "TITLE-ABS-KEY"),
  "DOCTYPE(er)", .op = "AND NOT"
)

# An object already in this schema is returned unchanged.
identical(scopus_records(example_records), example_records)
}
